\version "2.26.0"
% c129 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 102): 147-150 Hn 3 D4 with Hn 4 (was F4, re-attacked ff a second under Hn 1-2's G4; c119 moved the second from inside Hn 3-4 to Hn 2/Hn 3). The seventh F4 stays in Tbn 1, Bn 1, Vc, Vln I, Fl 1; Hn 3 D4 falls to C4 at 151.
% c126 (Adler PDF 175: careful consideration of the player's limits): Bsn 2 rests on 146.4 with the Cbsn breath (it played 135-158 without a rest, about 34 s; the B stays in Cbsn, Vc, Cb).
% c172 (harmony; A&S Unit 27, PDF 465-466 and Ex. 27-5: a 9th above V 'resolves to an octave within dominant harmony'; PDF 479 point 4: 9ths resolve down by step; PDF 470 sec. 8: B-D-F-A flat is V with a minor 9th and no root): 139-142 Hn 4 takes the stop chord's A flat3 down to G3 (was up an augmented second to B3), so the 9th resolves 9-8 in its own voice inside the dominant and the G area has its root from 139; B is no longer doubled above the B bass in the horns (c052). 143-146 Hn 4 D4 (a third under Hn 3's F4, not G3-F4). No voice reaches C: the stop chord still resolves to C only at 222-223.
% c367 (texture; Sebesky ch. 3, PDF 147: in a build "at least one voice is always moving upward"; PDF 140 rule 7 and PDF 145-146, Ex. 195-196: the lowest cello need not double the bass,
%   a second cello adds a chord tone; PDF 159, Ex. 207: a divided cello line carries melodic passing motion): 135-138 every line of the diminished-seventh chain fell in parallel
%   (strings, clarinets, bassoons, horns) under the pp < crescendo into the stop chord, and the cellos doubled the basses at the octave. The upper cellos now split from the bass
%   at 136 and rise D3-E3-G flat3-A flat3 (a chord tone of each chord, contrary to the falling bass), and take the stop chord's A flat3 down to G3 at 139 with Hn 4 (c172's 9-8);
%   the lower cellos keep D-D flat-C-B with the basses (c267: the bass stays doubled). "unis." for the F1 head at 140. No B added (c094), stop chord pitch classes and top B5 unchanged.
% RETRANSITION, bars 135-150. 135-138: a descending chain of diminished sevenths over the chromatic bass D - D flat - C - B,
% arriving on movement I's stop chord B-D-F-A flat at 138 (not resolved to C: that resolution belongs to the C-major arrival at 219).
% 139-146: a held G area with B natural in the bass (cyclic-notes, A&S PDF 449, 452-454): theme F1's head on G in imitation,
% horns 139, oboe and clarinet 141, violins 143, everyone 145. 147-150: V6/5 ff, allargando; the reprise comes from the inverted V.
% c026: 143-145 timpani: the scherzo's motive x (crotchet, two quavers, crotchet) on the dominant, quoted from the scherzo's own retransition
% (scherzo v04 bars 241, 243, 249, 251, 253-255; scherzo v24 241 and 249: b,4 b,8 b,8 b,4 on its dominant B), transposed down a major third to G (the C - A flat - E cycle
% run back). It is also the rhythm of F1's second bar (f'4 es'8 d'8 c'4), which Vln II (143) and Vln I (144) play in rhythmic unison with it.
% 145: two overlapping statements; 146 eighths; 147 roll (a written-out acceleration of the drum strokes).
% c073 (form; A&S PDF 112: a chord expanded over a whole section needs its root position; PDF 95, Ex. 6-4: the expanded V shapes the form; PDF 122: the bass of V6/5
% may leave the leading tone inside an expansion of V7): 147-150 the bass (Cb, Cbn, tuba, B.cl, Bn 2, Vc lower note) moves from B to the root G, so the ff dominant
% before the reprise stands on G for 147-153 (the i6/4 at 151 is a neighbour chord over it, PDF 129-130); B stays in the bass 138-146 (stop chord, stretto). The tonic root still comes only at 155 (V6/5 - i).

% c111 (melody; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 67-68: the flutes lead the top of the wood-wind octave layout): 150 Fl 1 restrikes D6 (was F6 tied from 149), joining Fl 2's held D6, so the top voice steps D6-C6 into F1's head at 151 (flutes a2 on C6) instead of leaping F6-C6; the 7th F stays in Ob 2, Tbn 1, Vln I and cellos.
% c508 (expression; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. IV, PDF 129: sf-p by orchestration, the strings ATTACK the wood-wind's piano chord sforzando, and Ex. 253 (PDF 418), where the strings' attack is itself sfp; PDF 130: the brass lead a crescendo chord into its climax): 149 the strings no longer hold the re-bowed chord ff through the bar, which in the wind chord's own register (Vln I F5/D6, Vln II B4/G5, Vla D4/G4, Vc G2/F3, Cb G1) covered the sfp < of the 21 wind, brass and timpani parts; they attack sf on the down-bow and swell p < ff on the up-bow with them, so the dip and the swell into 150 are heard; Tpt 1-2's ff strokes stay as the bar's sustained attack. Notes and bowing unchanged.
% c408 (expression; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. IV, PDF 129: an sf-p made by orchestration, the strings attacking while the wood-wind begin a piano chord; PDF 130: a crescendo grows from the strings through the wood-wind to the brass): 149 the wood-wind (Fl, Ob, C.a., Cl, B.cl., Bn, Cbsn) re-attack sfp < ff at 150 with c226's horns, Tpt 3, trombones, tuba and the timpani, instead of holding ff over that dip; the strings' re-bowed ff chord and Tpt 1-2's strokes give the attack. Notes unchanged.
% c526 (playability; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. I, PDF 37 and 44: wind players must breathe and be given a little rest): breath at the end of 146 for Fl 1, Ob 1, Cl 1 and Bn 1, after the staccato B, with the brass breath c136 put there; Ob 1, Cl 1 and Bn 1 played 141-150 (about 14 s, mf to ff) without a marked breath. Notes and dynamics unchanged.
retrFluteOne = { R1*4 | R1*4 | g''1\mf | aes''1 | g'''2->(\f d'''4. es'''8 | f'''4-.) es'''8( d'''8 c'''4-.) b''4-. \breathe | f'''1->\ff | d'''1 | f'''1\sfp\< | d'''2.\ff r4 | }
% c165 (MOLA PDF 5: logical cues during long rests; as c105): part-only cues before the retransition entries, which follow 29-140 bars of rest:
% Vln I's F1 head at 145-146, an octave lower so it stays near the stave, for the trumpets, Ob 2, Fl 2 and the cor anglais (147 ff; the cor anglais has rested since bar 6), the cellos at 145-146 for the
% tuba, at 141-142 for the trombones (143 pp) and at 142-143 for the bass clarinet (144 p, its first note since bar 6). Score and MIDI unchanged.
retrFluteTwo = { R1*4 | R1*6 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \transposedCueDuring "vlnOne" #UP c { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | d'''1->\ff | b''1 | d'''1~\sfp\< | d'''2.\ff r4 | }
% c028: 138 the sf of the stop chord decays (sf >) in the strings and oboe 1, so the winds' sub. p chord is heard under it (Adler II PDF 190, Ex. XV-35: held strings sf > pp; forte subito piano needs the loud chord to give way)
retrOboeOne = { R1*3 | b''1\sf\> | R1*2\! | g''2->(\mf d''4. es''8 | f''4-.) es''8( d''8 c''4-.) b'4-. | f''1 | f''1 | g''2->(\f d''4. es''8 | f''4-.) es''8( d''8 c''4-.) b'4-. \breathe | b''1->\ff | g''1 | b''1~\sfp\< | b''2.\ff r4 | }
% c010: 138 Ob 1 B5 sf on top of the stop chord, as movement I puts B5 on top (26, 138); the B resolves only at 218-219 (A&S PDF 597-598).
% c119 (Rimsky PDF 90, rule 4: concords, not discords, to two instruments of one kind, "specially ... the oboe"): Ob 2 a sixth under Ob 1 (was F5 under B5, a tritone)
retrOboeTwo = { R1*4 | R1*6 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \transposedCueDuring "vlnOne" #UP c { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | d''1->\ff | b'1 | d''1~\sfp\< | d''2.\ff r4 | }
retrCorAnglais = { R1*4 | R1*6 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \transposedCueDuring "vlnOne" #UP c { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | d''1->\ff | b'1 | d''1~\sfp\< | d''2.\ff r4 | }
% c308 (expression; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. IV, PDF 130: a prolonged crescendo adds strings, then wood-wind, then brass; PDF 129: when the wood-wind begins a piano chord the strings attack it
%   sforzando, "a compound chord for preference"): 135-137 the strings start the diminished-seventh chain pp < alone, as they ended 133-134; Cl 1-2 and Bn 1-2 join p < at 136, Hn 3-4 p < at 137,
%   so the crescendo into the stop chord is built by groups. 138 Vln I and Vln II take the stop chord as double stops non div. (A flat4-F5, F4-D5), so the strings' sf is a compound chord
%   over the winds' sub. p. Pitches of every line, the pp < into sf > of c028/c198, the stopped horns at 138 and the winds' sub. p unchanged.
retrClOne = { R1 | bes'1\p\< | a'1 | aes'1\subP | R1*2 | g''2->(\mf d''4. es''8 | f''4-.) es''8( d''8 c''4-.) b'4-. | d''1 | d''1 | g'2->(\f d'4. es'8 | f'4-.) es'8( d'8 c'4-.) b4-. \breathe | g''1->\ff | f''1 | g''1~\sfp\< | g''2.\ff r4 | }
% c364 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 90 rule 4, PDF 91-92: in widely divided harmony each pair of one colour takes a concord, the pairs in normal register order): 136-138 Cl 2 G4-F#4-F4 (was E4-E♭4-D4) and Bn 1 E3-E♭3-D3 (was G3-G♭3-F3), so the clarinets and the bassoons each move in minor thirds instead of tritones; same pitch classes, same chain.
retrClTwo = { R1 | g'1\p\< | fis'1 | f'1\subP | R1*8 | d''1->\ff | b'1 | d''1~\sfp\< | d''2.\ff r4 | }
% c139 (Rimsky PDF 127, Ex. 245-250: in a crescendo the added group enters p and grows faster; PDF 136: a group silent for a while gains fresh interest; Ex. 265/273-274: Cl. basso doubling the bassoon in a held bass): 144-146 B. cl. doubles Bn 2's held B1, entering p under the crescendo, before its ff at 147. (Doubling from 135 adds the B. cl. staff to the 133-143 system and pushes the score to 17 pages.)
% c427 (parts book 'crescendo too small' at 149 since c408): part-only minimum hairpin length with spacing rods, as at intro 10 (score and MIDI unchanged).
retrBassCl = { R1*4 | R1*3 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vc" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | b,1\p\< | b,1\f | b,2. r4 | g,1->\ff | g,1 | \tag #'part { \once \override Staff.Hairpin.minimum-length = #6 \once \override Staff.Hairpin.springs-and-rods = #ly:spanner::set-spacing-rods } g,1~\sfp\< | g,2.\ff r4 | }
% c166 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. I, PDF 37, 44: rests for breathing): breath at the end of 140 for Bn 1-2 and Hn 3-4, which held 135-146 (17 s, pp < f) without one; the strings (Vla, Cb) and the cellos' F1 head carry 140-141.
% c347 (texture; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. III, PDF 108: in soft four-part harmony of horns and bassoons 'the concords being given to the horns, the discords to the bassoons', and 'bassoons may also be written inside the horns';
%   PDF 113: strings and brass have no affinity of tone and are seldom combined by crossing or enclosure): 141-142 the four horns hold the concords G3-D4-B4-D5 and Bn 1 doubled Hn 3's D4,
%   so the seventh F4 of V7 over the B bass sounded only in the violas, enclosed by the horns. Bn 1 now takes F4 with the violas, inside the horns' D4-B4 gap, and returns to F3 at 143. Notes elsewhere, dynamics and breaths unchanged.
retrBsnOne = { R1 | e1\p\< | es1 | d1\subP | f1 | f1 \breathe | f'1\cresc | f'1 | f1 | f1 | g2->(\f d4. es8 | f4-.) es8( d8 c4-.) b,4-. \breathe | f1->\ff | g1 | f1~\sfp\< | f2.\ff r4 | }
% c256 (playability; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. I, PDF 37 and 44: wind players must be given a little rest from time to time): Bn 2 drops its pickup D3 at 150.4 and breathes with Bn 1;
%   it played 147-158 ff in the low register (about 17 s) with no rest. The pickup stays in Vc, Cbsn, B.cl., tuba and basses.
retrBsnTwo = { R1 | des1\p\< | c1 | b,1\subP | b,1 | b,1 \breathe | b,1\cresc | b,1 | b,1 | b,1 | b,1\f | b,2. r4 | g,1->\ff | g,1 | g,1~\sfp\< | g,2.\ff r4 | }
retrCbsn = { R1*4 | R1*4 | b,,1\pp\cresc~ | b,,1~ | b,,1\f~ | b,,1 \breathe | g,,1->\ff | g,,1 | g,,1~\sfp\< | g,,2.\ff r4 | }
% c114 (orchestration; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 115-116: a trumpet figure over held harmony needs harmony of another colour, and PDF 51: horn = half a trumpet): 148-150 Hn 1 G4 (a2 with Hn 2; was D5 held ff, the pitch Tpt 1 restrikes at 148 and 149), so the trumpets' strokes are not covered by a held brass unison.
% c117 (texture; Adler ch. 15, PDF 176: in a combination 'each theme must be heard at good strength'; PDF 184: the principal voice is marked louder than the subordinate ones): 143-144 Hn 1-2 play F1's head an octave below Vln I's f entry, but were still at the p of 139/141; now p < (142) f at 143, so the head is a string-and-brass doubling at one dynamic (f at 145 now implied).
% c136 (playability; Adler ch. 10, PDF 304: wide leaps are hard, the hornist must 'hear' and 'find' each note; PDF 284: soft attacks high up are the hardest): 141 Hn 1 enters its held D5 (written A5) p on beat 2, not straight from the head's B3 on 140.4 (a tenth with an eighth's gap). 146 brass breath before the ff V6/5 (PDF 292: loud slow passages need more breath), as the contrabassoon already has.
% c226 (playability, brass attacks; Adler ch. 9, adler1 PDF 290, Ex. IX-9: brass 'sff>pp<fff' on a held chord; PDF 307, Ex. X-54 (trumpet) and adler2 PDF 8, Ex. X-87 (tuba): sff>p<ff): 149 the held brass (Hn 1-4, Tpt 3, Tbn 1-2, B. tbn, tuba) re-attack the dominant sfp and swell to ff at 150 under the allargando, instead of four flat ff bars; Tpt 1-2 strokes, woodwinds and strings stay ff; no pitch changed.
retrHnOne = { R1*4 | g'2->(\p d'4. es'8 | f'4-.) es'8( d'8 c'4-.) b4-. | \partCombineApart r4 d''2. | \partCombineAutomatic d''1\< | g'2->(\f d'4. es'8 | f'4-.) es'8( d'8 c'4-.) b4-. | g'1 | f'1 \breathe | d''1->\ff | g'1 | g'1~\sfp\< | g'2.\ff r4 | }
% c052 (harmony; A&S Unit 25, PDF 423, 439: the leading tone is not doubled): 147-150 Hn 2 and the violas take G instead of B above the bass B.
retrHnTwo = { R1*4 | R1*2 | \partCombineApart g'1\p | \partCombineAutomatic g'1\< | g'2->(\f d'4. es'8 | f'4-.) es'8( d'8 c'4-.) b4-. | d'1 | d'1 \breathe | g'1->\ff | g'1 | g'1~\sfp\< | g'2.\ff r4 | }
% c088 (expression; Adler ch. 10, PDF 308: stopped horn is used for single notes and is effective 'when one wishes to have the horn play sf>p', marked + and then o for open): 138 Hn 3-4 take the stop chord's D and A flat stopped sf> with the strings' and oboe's sf>, open p at 139 (were sub. p under the strings' sf).
% c119 (Rimsky PDF 102: discords of the seventh or second go to instruments of different colour): Hn 3-4 a third F4/D4 at 147-150 (was the second G4/F4); Hn 3 keeps its prepared F4
% c174 (orchestration; Casanelles ch. 6, PDF 286-287: 'the fewer the instruments in a frequency range, the clearer and more present they become ... sounds in the same frequency range mask each other'; PDF 277-278: ensemble timbre of instruments at the same pitch):
% 143-144 Hn 3-4 rest (were F4/D4 crescendo, held inside Hn 1-2's a2 F1 head G4-B3 in the same colour: unisons on its D4 and F4, seconds against its G4 and E flat 4, crossed by its C4-B3); they reach mf at 142 and re-enter f at 145 on F4/D4. The held G-B-D-F stays in Tbn 1-2/B.tbn (D4/B3/G2 pp <), Vla D4, Cl 1 D5, Ob 1 F5, Bn 1 F3 and the B bass.
% c397 (texture; Adler part 2 ch. 11, PDF 44 and Ex. XI-24, PDF 45-47 (Bartok, Concerto for Orchestra i 313-386): a brass fugato passes the subject between brass groups,
%   each entry 'f, ben marc.', and in the stretto the horn pairs enter a2 in turn ('the composer equates the strength of two horns in unison with that of one trumpet or one trombone');
%   PDF 42 (Hindemith, Ex. XI-23): every entry in the players' best register 'so that the subject comes through clearly every time it is played'):
%   145-146 Hn 3-4 a2 play the stretto's last F1-head entry at G4 (with Cl 1 and the violas) instead of holding F4/D4, so the horn pairs alternate (Hn 1 139, Hn 1-2 143, Hn 3-4 145)
%   and the entry's G4 octave is no longer under six held brass notes in its own register. V7 stays complete in the held brass (Hn 1 G4-F4, Hn 2 D4, Tbn 1 F4, Tbn 2 G3, B.tbn G2).
%   Same entry bar, rhythm and dynamic as the other parts; c175's rest at 143-144 and re-entry f at 145 kept; 147-150 unchanged. The Hn 3-4 entry is marked "ben marc." as at 37 (c297).
% c527 (texture; Adler part 2 ch. 17, PDF 264 (Mozart never uses the orchestral horns while the solo horn plays) and PDF 274 (keep the accompanying forces registrally distant from the soloist)): 139-140 Hn 3 rests under Hn 1's solo F1 head (its held D4 was the head's D4 on 139.3 and lay inside its F4-E flat4-D4-C4-B3 descent at 140, same colour and level, the clash c174 removed at 143-144); Hn 4 keeps the stop chord's A flat3 (+) -> G3 (o) resolution (c088, c172) below the head; Hn 3 re-enters p < open on D4 at 141, Hn 4 restates p < there after its breath. D stays in the violas.
retrHnThree = { R1*2 | es'1\p\< | d'1\sf\>-\stopped | \partCombineSoloII R1*2\! \partCombineAutomatic | d'1\p\<-\open | d'1\mf | R1*2 | g'2->(\f^\markup \italic "ben marc." d'4. es'8 | f'4-.) es'8( d'8 c'4-.) b4-. \breathe | d'1->\ff | d'1 | d'1~\sfp\< | d'2.\ff r4 | }
retrHnFour = { R1*2 | a1\p\< | aes1\sf\>-\stopped | g1\p-\open | g1 \breathe | g1\p\< | g1\mf | R1*2 | g'2->(\f^\markup \italic "ben marc." d'4. es'8 | f'4-.) es'8( d'8 c'4-.) b4-. \breathe | d'1->\ff | d'1 | d'1~\sfp\< | d'2.\ff r4 | }
% c111 (melody/rhythm; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 71-72: fanfare figures built from the natural scale, given rhythm, are the brass's own melodic material, in the middle and upper register of trumpets and horns;
% Ex. 70, PDF 242: trumpets and horns move in fanfare rhythm over held wind and string chords; Dvorak 9/iv 204-207 (corpus, MusicXML numbering): a held ff wind chord is articulated by half-note restrikes in the low strings and single fz half-note trumpet strokes rising one chord tone per bar, before the fff entry at 208):
% 148-150 Tpt 1-2 strike the dominant's natural-scale tones in half notes over the held tutti (Tpt 1 D5-F5 | D5-F5 | G5, Tpt 2 B4-D5 | B4-D5 | D5), so the four-bar V is not a static block, the line peaks on G5 in the allargando bar
% and the allargando at 149-150 is heard in the spacing of the strokes. Every move at a barline is contrary or oblique to the flutes', oboes' and violins' alternation (no octaves). Harmony, bass G and Tpt 3 unchanged.
% c240 (cyclic lens; Adler ch. 15, adler2 PDF 189-190, Ex. XV-35, bar 2: "a short sforzando trumpet note combined with long notes in the strings"): 138 Tpt 1 B4 and Tpt 2 F4,
%   an eighth, sf, over the strings' held sf > stop chord: movement I's trumpet pitches in both of its stop chords (I 26 and 138: Tpt 1 B4, Tpt 2 F4). The B4 is cut off unresolved;
%   the trumpets' next B4 (Tpt 2, 147) goes to D, and Tpt 1 B4 -> C5 is still heard only at 222 -> 223.
retrTptOne = { R1 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \transposedCueDuring "vlnOne" #UP c { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | b'8\sf r8 r4 r2 | R1*6 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \transposedCueDuring "vlnOne" #UP c { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | d''1->\ff | d''2-> f''2-> | d''2-> f''2-> | g''2.-> r4 | }
retrTptTwo = { R1 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \transposedCueDuring "vlnOne" #UP c { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | f'8\sf r8 r4 r2 | R1*6 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \transposedCueDuring "vlnOne" #UP c { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | b'1->\ff | b'2-> d''2-> | b'2-> d''2-> | d''2.-> r4 | }
retrTptThree = { R1*4 | R1*6 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \transposedCueDuring "vlnOne" #UP c { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | g'1->\ff | g'1 | g'1~\sfp\< | g'2.\ff r4 | }
retrTbnOne = { R1*4 | R1*2 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vc" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | d'1\pp\cresc | d'1 | f'1\f | f'1 \breathe | f'1->\ff | f'1 | f'1~\sfp\< | f'2.\ff r4 | }  % c468 (MOLA PDF 7, 8 and Example 7, PDF 12; c268, c405): the trombones' and bass trombone's pp crescendo at 143-145 crossed the 143|144 page turn as a bare wedge; now "cresc." like Cbsn, Bn, timpani and strings in the same build, restated "(cresc.)" after the turn. MIDI unchanged.
% c374 (orchestration; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. III, PDF 100: 'part writing should be of the close order with no empty spaces in the intervals'; PDF 101: the low trombone and the bass in octaves, the other parts above): 145-146 Tbn 2 keeps G3 from 143-144 (was D4, a unison with Hn 4 and the violas), so the f V7 in the brass is G2-G3-D4-F4-G4 instead of G2 with nothing up to four instruments on D4/F4 (a twelfth empty); it moves to D4 at 147 with the ff as before. 147-150 (protected, c194) unchanged.
retrTbnTwo = { R1*4 | R1*2 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vc" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | g1\pp\cresc | g1 | g1\f | g1 \breathe | d'1->\ff | d'1 | d'1~\sfp\< | d'2.\ff r4 | }
retrBassTbn = { R1*4 | R1*2 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vc" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | g,1\pp\cresc | g,1 | g,1\f | g,1 \breathe | g,1->\ff | g,1 | \tag #'part { \once \override Staff.Hairpin.minimum-length = #6 \once \override Staff.Hairpin.springs-and-rods = #ly:spanner::set-spacing-rods } g,1~\sfp\< | g,2.\ff r4 | }  % c525: part-only hairpin length at 149, which now starts a line in the bass trombone part (as at 10, c315)
retrTuba = { R1*4 | R1*6 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vc" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | g,,1->\ff | g,,1 | g,,1~\sfp\< | g,,2.\ff r4 | }
% c045 (Adler part 2 PDF 80, Ex. XII-48: a longer roll is written as tied notes; an untied roll restarts at each barline): rolls on one pitch tied across the barlines
% c060 (cyclic lens; Sebesky ch. 1, PDF 13-14: focus): 143 the scherzo's motive x starts mf (was pp) so it is heard against Vln I's f F1 head; crescendo to f at 145 unchanged
retrTimp = { R1*4 | R1*4 | g,4\mf\cresc g,8 g,8 g,4 r4 | g,4 g,8 g,8 g,4 r4 | g,4\f g,8 g,8 g,4 g,8 g,8 | g,8 g,8 g,8 g,8 g,8 g,8 g,8 g,8 | g,1:32\ff~ | g,1:32 | g,1:32\sfp\<~ | g,2.:32\ff r4 | }  % c346 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 48: a roll can make the sfp; PDF 41: brass sf > p): the roll re-attacks sfp < at 149 with c226's horns, Tpt 3, trombones and tuba, instead of holding ff over their dip
% c118 (Adler ch. 2, PDF 38, Ex. II-20 Beethoven Coriolanus: a held loud note gets down-bow and up-bow so the next attack is solid): 149-150 Vln I-II and violas \dnup on the tied ff chord
% (about 2.8 s under the allargando), so 151's ff F1 starts on a fresh down-bow; Vc and Cb change bow at will (their pickup D on beat 4 stays an up-bow).
% c318 (expression; Adler ch. 2, PDF 38, Ex. II-19, II-20): 147 Vln I-II down-bow on the ff V6/5 (alternation after 146's staccato B put it on an up-bow; the down-up at 149 assumes a down-bow at 147).
% c399 (expression; Casanelles, The Hyperorchestra ch. 6, PDF 276: the spectral envelope of a note changes with "the amount of pressure applied to the bow or its location";
%   PDF 272-273, Fig. 6.1 (Mahler 1/i): a sound heard from afar has lost its upper partials first; PDF 277: soft strings and flute-like colours merge): 135-138 all strings start the falling
%   diminished-seventh chain sul tasto at pp and move the bow back to the normal place during the crescendo, "ord." on the sf stop chord at 138, so the chain grows in brightness as well as
%   in loudness (from veiled to full bite) while Cl/Bn (136) and Hn 3-4 (137) join. The score's first gradual change of bow position (until now it only switches from one note to the next, e.g. sul tasto at 43, 171, 183 and ord. at 67, 181, 199).
%   Text only: notes, bowings, dynamics, slurs and MIDI unchanged. Engraving: font-size -1, the text set after the up-bow sign on the same line (left padding 2.7, no horizontal
%   skyline padding), and the existing "div."/"non div." folded into the spanner texts, so page 7 keeps its height (a stacked line in each string staff compressed it by 5.2 staff-spaces).
retrTastoOrd = { \once \override TextSpanner.font-size = #-1 \once \override TextSpanner.outside-staff-padding = #0.25 \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.padding = #2.7 \once \override TextSpanner.outside-staff-horizontal-padding = #0 \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.text = \markup \italic "sul tasto" \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.right.text = \markup \italic "ord." }
% the same, with the existing "non div." (Vln I-II at 138) and "div." (violas at 135) folded into the spanner texts, so no extra line of text is stacked (page 7 stays uncompressed)
retrTastoOrdND = { \once \override TextSpanner.font-size = #-1 \once \override TextSpanner.outside-staff-padding = #0.25 \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.padding = #2.7 \once \override TextSpanner.outside-staff-horizontal-padding = #0 \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.text = \markup \italic "sul tasto" \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.right.text = \markup \italic "ord., non div." }
retrTastoOrdDiv = { \once \override TextSpanner.font-size = #-1 \once \override TextSpanner.outside-staff-padding = #0.25 \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.padding = #3.6 \once \override TextSpanner.outside-staff-horizontal-padding = #0 \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.text = \markup \italic "div., sul tasto" \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.right.text = \markup \italic "ord." }
retrVlnOne = { \retrTastoOrdND aes''1\pp\<\upbow\startTextSpan | g''1 | ges''1 | <aes' f''>1\sf\>\stopTextSpan | R1*4\! | g''2->(\f d''4. es''8 | f''4-.) es''8( d''8 c''4-.) r4 | g'''2->( d'''4. es'''8 | f'''4-.) es'''8( d'''8 c'''4-.) b''4-. | <f'' d'''>1->\ff\downbow | <d'' b''>1 | <f'' d'''>1\sfp\<\dnup~ | <f'' d'''>2.\ff r4 | }
% c094 (orchestration; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. III, PDF 82 note: the bass is doubled in the lower octave only and 'should never mix with the others'; PDF 83-84: the bass of a dominant inversion or diminished seventh is not doubled in an upper part): 135-138 Vln II takes the F-E-E flat-D line an octave above Cl 2/Hn 3/lower violas (was D5-D flat5-C5-B4, the bass two octaves up); at the stop chord 138 B is now only in the bass and Ob 1's protected B5.
retrVlnTwo = { \retrTastoOrdND f''1\pp\<\upbow\startTextSpan | e''1 | es''1 | <f' d''>1\sf\>\stopTextSpan | R1\! | R1 | R1 | g'2->(\mf d'4. es'8 | f'4-.) es'8( d'8 c'4-.) b4-. | b'1\f | g''2->( d''4. es''8 | f''4-.) es''8( d''8 c''4-.) b'4-. | <b' g''>1->\ff\downbow | <g' f''>1 | <b' g''>1\sfp\<\dnup~ | <b' g''>2.\ff r4 | }
retrVla = { \retrTastoOrdDiv <f' b'>1\pp\<\upbow\startTextSpan | <e' bes'>1 | <es' a'>1 | <d' aes'>1\sf\>\stopTextSpan | d'1\p^\markup \italic "unis." | d'1 | f'1\cresc | f'1 | d'1 | d'1 | g'2->(\f d'4. es'8 | f'4-.) es'8( d'8 c'4-.) b4-. | <d' g'>1->\ff | <d' g'>1 | <d' g'>1\sfp\<\dnup~ | <d' g'>2.\ff r4 | }
retrVc = { \retrTastoOrd d1\pp\<\upbow\startTextSpan \tag #'part \noBreak | <des e>1^\markup \italic "div." | <c ges>1 | <b, aes>1\sf\>\stopTextSpan | <b, g>1\p | g2->(^\markup \italic "unis." d4. es8 | f4-.) es8( d8 c4-.) b,4-. | b,1\cresc~\cbaw | b,1~ | b,1~ | b,1\f~ | b,1 | <g, f>1->\ff | <g, g>1 | <g, f>1~\sfp\<\cbawN | <g, f>2.\ff r4 | }
retrCb = { \retrTastoOrd d,1\pp\<\upbow\startTextSpan | des,1 | c,1 | b,,1\sf\>\stopTextSpan | b,,1\p~ | b,,1~\cbawN | b,,1\cresc~ | b,,1~ | b,,1~ | b,,1~ | b,,1\f~ | b,,1 | g,,1->\ff | g,,1 | g,,1~\sfp\<\cbawN | g,,2.\ff r4 | }
